{"name":"Royal Marquis","hex":"#545e97","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"royal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to a monarch or his (or her) family.","Having the air or demeanour of a monarch; illustrious; magnanimous; of more than common size or excellence."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A royal person; a member of a royal family.","A standard size of printing paper, measuring 25 by 20 inches."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/royal"},{"term":"marquis","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A nobleman in England, France, and Germany, of a rank next below that of duke, but above a count. Originally, the marquis was an officer whose duty was to guard the marches or frontiers of the kingdom. The office has ceased, and the name is now a mere title conferred by letters patent or letters close.","Any of various nymphalid butterflies of the Asian genus Bassarona (or Euthalia)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/marquis"}],"entries":[{"title":"Marquess","blocks":[{"text":"A marquess is a nobleman of high hereditary rank in various European peerages and in those of some of their former colonies. The German-language equivalent is Markgraf (margrave) and the Italian-language equivalent Marchese. A woman with the rank of a marquess or the wife of a marquess is a marchioness or marquise. These titles are also used to translate equivalent Asian styles, as in Imperial China and Imperial Japan."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/William_Kerr%2C_4th_Marquess_of_Lothian_on_a_charger%2C_by_David_Morier.jpg/330px-William_Kerr%2C_4th_Marquess_of_Lothian_on_a_charger%2C_by_David_Morier.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marquess","wikidata":"Q209726"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Pompa_funebris_Albert_Ardux_-_Trazegnies.jpg/500px-P